%I A400610 #23 Oct 04 2026 15:03:11
%S A400610 1,4,70,20,1112,100,10120,10,11110,1001110,10000,10000100,1001110000,
%T A400610 1000010,2000000002,1100001010,1000010000,100000000201,1000001000020,
%U A400610 1000010000000,100000000000120,1000000001000,1000002000000010,100000000001,1000000001000000,10000000010,100000000001000
%N A400610 a(n) is the least positive integer k such that digsum(k') = n * digsum(k), where k' = A003415(k) is the arithmetic derivative of k and digsum(x) = A007953(x) is the sum of decimal digits of x.
%C A400610 All terms are in A400611.
%C A400610 Such k always exists.  Thus digsum(A003415(10^m)) = digsum(7*m). It can be shown that for all n > 1 there exists m with digsum(7*m) = n, and thus k = 10^m works.
%C A400610 a(18) <= 1000001000020, a(19) <= 1000010000000.
%C A400610 From _David A. Corneth_, Oct 02 2026: (Start)
%C A400610 Records of A003415(n)/n occur at powers of 2 (cf. A000079).
%C A400610 By definition of the arithmetic derivative of a number (A003415), the records of A003415(n)/n lie in the positive integers (division by 0 is not defined).
%C A400610 The definition of the arithmetic derivative is If n = Product p_i^e_i, a(n) = n * Sum (e_i/p_i).
%C A400610 Using this the domain of records can be reduced to numbers all of whose prime divisors are consecutive primes starting at 2 (cf. A055932).
%C A400610 As a(n) = n * Sum (e_i/p_i) we have a(n)/n = Sum (e_i/p_i). This has primes in the denominators of terms. Minimizing primes gives that numbers must come from A055932.
%C A400610 We may further restrict that n must be a Least integer of its prime signature (Cf. A025487). We have sums of e_i/p_i. That sum is maximized when the largest exponents are divided by the smallest primes. This happens exactly when n is in A025487.
%C A400610 We may further restrict this so that n has at most one prime factor. Choosing all p_i minimal i.e. 2 we get that n must be a power of 2. This maximizes Sum (e_i/p_i) hence the ratio A003415(n)/n. (End)
%H A400610 David A. Corneth, <a href="/A400610/a400610.gp.txt">PARI program</a>
%e A400610 a(6) = 10120 because 10120' = 18564 has digital sum 24, while 10120 has digital sum 4 = 24/6, and no smaller number works.
%p A400610 ader:= proc(n) local F,t;
%p A400610       F:= ifactors(n)[2];
%p A400610       n*add(t[2]/t[1],t=F)
%p A400610 end proc;
%p A400610 ader(0):= 0: ader(1):= 0:
%p A400610 ds:= n -> convert(convert(n,base,10),`+`):
%p A400610 V:= Array(0..20): V[0]:= 1: count:= 1:
%p A400610 for n from 2 while count < 21 do
%p A400610 r:= ds(ader(n))/ds(n);
%p A400610 if r::posint and r <= 20 and V[r] = 0 then
%p A400610     V[r]:= n; count:= count+1
%p A400610 fi
%p A400610 od:
%p A400610 convert(V,list);
%o A400610 (PARI) \\ See Corneth link
%Y A400610 Cf. A003415, A007953, A400611.
%K A400610 nonn,base,new
%O A400610 0,2
%A A400610 _Robert Israel_, Oct 01 2026
%E A400610 More terms from _David A. Corneth_, Oct 02 2026
